{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Chicago & Alton's Kansas City lines \u2014 the half of the Alton Route this map was missing. The Chicago\u2013St. Louis main line is already drawn in alton_gmo_abraham_lincoln.geojson; this layer adds what branched off it: Springfield\u2013Jacksonville\u2013Roodhouse, Roodhouse\u2013Louisiana, Mo.\u2013Mexico, Mo. (the leased Louisiana & Missouri River Railroad), Mexico\u2013Slater\u2013Kansas City (the leased Kansas City, St. Louis & Chicago Railroad) and the Godfrey\u2013Roodhouse line that carried the St. Louis\u2013Kansas City trains. Together these were about half of the Alton's 1,053 route miles. Added 2026-08-27. Split into three features: the Chicago\u2013Kansas City train under the Chicago & Alton and its Alton Railroad successor, the St. Louis\u2013Kansas City train, and the cut-back Bloomington\u2013Kansas City motorcar of the Gulf, Mobile & Ohio years. All three carry #5B7C99, the colour this repo already uses for the Alton and for the GM&O (CLAUDE.md Rule 4).", "not_yet_sourced": "The year through Chicago\u2013Kansas City passenger service ended is not sourced; 1947, the GM&O purchase, is used as the boundary only because the documented GM&O-era service is the Bloomington\u2013Kansas City motorcar rather than a Chicago train. The 1960 end of that motorcar is the date of the last photograph found of it, not a discontinuance notice. Whether the GM&O continued the St. Louis\u2013Kansas City train after 1947 has not been checked. Roodhouse local history says passenger service to the town ended \"in the 1950s\", which is consistent but too vague to date a segment with. Every headway_min and avg_speed_kmh in this file is an estimate.", "source": "Wikipedia (Alton Railroad, Gateway Western Railway, Roodhouse, Illinois), american-rails.com, Trains Magazine GM&O photo features", "pulled": "20260827"}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-87.6659905, 41.839197